    "q-111789": {
      "dom_id": "step-q111789",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Let\n$T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, and yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ and $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the point $(2,-1),$ which is defined by the (repeated) columns of the matrix $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{-1-0}{2-0}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
